ZSNES 2 Debian packaging notes
==============================

Package identity and coexistence
--------------------------------

The Debian source and binary package names are intentionally `zsnes2`, not
`zsnes`. ZSNES 2 is packaged as a separate maintained continuation and must be
co-installable with the historical `zsnes` package.

The package therefore does not declare `Provides`, `Replaces`, `Breaks`, or
`Conflicts` against `zsnes`. It installs the executable as `/usr/bin/zsnes2`,
uses zsnes2-specific desktop, AppStream, icon, and manual-page names, and
builds with `ZCONF="zsnes2"`.

This prevents ZSNES 2 from adopting the legacy `~/.zsnes` directory and gives
it its own XDG configuration directory, `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zsnes2` (normally
`~/.config/zsnes2`).

Architecture
------------

Upstream ZSNES 2.3.4 completed the migration of the former 32-bit x86 assembly
implementation to C11 and now supports multiple CPU architectures.

The Debian package currently follows the Linux architectures explicitly
supported by upstream:

- amd64
- i386
- arm64
- riscv64

32-bit ARM (`armhf`) is deliberately not enabled by this packaging revision.
Upstream does not currently provide a 32-bit ARM Makefile target. armhf should
only be enabled together with a tested upstream build configuration for that
architecture.

Hardening and PIE
-----------------

The package follows upstream's architecture-specific position-independence
policy.

On amd64 and i386, upstream uses absolute addressing and explicitly links
non-PIE. Debian PIE hardening is therefore disabled on those architectures,
while the remaining Debian hardening features stay enabled.

On arm64 and riscv64, upstream requires position-independent code and does not
disable PIE. These architectures therefore retain the full Debian hardening
configuration, including PIE.

SDL3 and audio
--------------

The package declares SDL3 directly in Build-Depends.

The package disables upstream's optional PipeWire and libao autodetection at
build time and uses SDL3 as the deterministic audio backend. This avoids build
results changing according to whichever optional development package happens
to be installed in the build environment.

Rendering
---------

OpenGL support remains enabled, while the SDL software renderer is also
available. A discrete GPU is therefore not a package requirement.
